{
  "id": "course-schedule-iv",
  "questionId": "course-schedule-iv",
  "slug": "course-schedule-iv",
  "title": "Course Schedule IV",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Topological sort",
  "patterns": [
    "topological sort",
    "graph",
    "reachability"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "answering 'can u reach v' by re-searching from scratch for every single query is wasteful when many queries share the same graph - can you precompute reachability once?",
    "process the courses in topological order; when you finish course u, you already know everything reachable from every one of u's own prerequisites",
    "maintain a reach[v] set (or bit array) for every node; for each edge u -> v processed in topological order, reach[v] absorbs u itself plus everything already in reach[u]",
    "after processing the whole topological order, answering a query [u, v] is just checking whether v is in reach[u]"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "checking the edge direction backwards, i.e. checking if v reaches u instead of u reaches v",
    "answering u == v queries as true by assuming a node is trivially a prerequisite of itself",
    "recomputing a full BFS or DFS per query instead of precomputing reachability once, which is correct but far slower than necessary and can time out with many queries"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "There are `numCourses` courses, labeled 0 to numCourses - 1. You're given\na list of `prerequisites` pairs, where each pair [a, b] means course a is\na (possibly indirect) prerequisite for course b, a must be completed\nbefore b, directly or through a chain of other courses.\n\nYou're also given a list of `queries`, where each query [u, v] asks whether u is a prerequisite of v.\n\n**Return an array of the same length as `queries`, where each entry is 1 if the answer to that query is true, and 0 if it is false.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= numCourses <= 100",
      "each prerequisite pair [a, b] has 0 <= a, b < numCourses and a != b",
      "the prerequisites are guaranteed to form a DAG (no cycles)",
      "1 <= queries.length"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a query where u equals v - false, unless an explicit self-referencing chain exists (which cannot happen in a valid DAG)",
      "no prerequisites at all - every query answers 0",
      "a long transitive chain that a query must follow through several intermediate courses",
      "multiple queries reusing the same pair of nodes"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "numCourses = 5, prerequisites = [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4]], queries = [[0, 4], [4, 0], [1, 3], [3, 0]]",
        "output": "[1, 0, 1, 0]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 0, 1, 0]",
        "explanation": "0 -> 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 is a chain, so 0 is a prerequisite of 4 and 1 is a prerequisite of 3, but nothing points backward toward 0"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "numCourses = 3, prerequisites = [[0, 1], [0, 2]], queries = [[0, 1], [0, 2], [1, 2], [2, 1]]",
        "output": "[1, 1, 0, 0]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 1, 0, 0]",
        "explanation": "0 is a prerequisite of both 1 and 2, but 1 and 2 have no relationship to each other"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "numCourses = 4, prerequisites = [], queries = [[0, 1], [2, 3]]",
